<title>SWITZERLAND: WTO sets up panel on Hungarian farm subsidies.</title>
<headline>WTO sets up panel on Hungarian farm subsidies.</headline>
<dateline>GENEVA 1997-02-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>The World Trade Organisation (WTO) set up a neutral panel to rule on complaints against Hungary's farm export subsidies brought by Australia, New Zealand, the United States and Argentina, a WTO spokesman said.</p>
<p>Four other countries -- Canada, Japan, Thailand and Uruguay -- reserved their third-party rights in the dispute, according to trade sources at the WTO's Dispute Settlement Body.</p>
<p>Hungary is accused of breaching agricultural export subsidy curbs set during the Uruguay Round trade negotiations which ended in December 1993 under WTO's predecessor body, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT).</p>
<p>Hungary has claimed the limits imposed are based on wrong export subsidy figures for the period between 1986-1990, supplied by the then communist government. On Thursday, it reiterated the problem had been caused by an erroneous schedule.</p>
</text>
